\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Integrating and conditioning the matter field}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the \vocabk{joint law}{fdip-0076}, let
#{\cW(U)=\frac12\log\det_{\mathbb R}Q_U} and set
##{Z_U=\int_{G^E}\ue^{-S_W(U)-\cW(U)}\,d\mu(U).}
Then
##{\mathfrak Z=(2\pi)^{N/2}Z_U,\qquad
 d\mathbb P_U(U)=Z_U^{-1}\ue^{-S_W(U)-\cW(U)}\,d\mu(U).}
A [conditional matter kernel](fdip-003A) is
##{K_U(d\phi)=(2\pi)^{-N/2}(\det Q_U)^{1/2}
 \ue^{-\frac12\langle\phi,Q_U\phi\rangle}
 \,d\operatorname{vol}(\phi),}
so, conditionally on #{U}, the field has law
#{N(0,Q_U^{-1})}. In particular the induced action adds to the bare
gauge action exactly, with no large-mass approximation.}

\proof{
\p{The [real Gaussian integral](fdip-0070), converted from its
normalized reference volume to ordinary volume, gives
##{\int_{\mathcal H}\ue^{-\frac12\langle\phi,Q_U\phi\rangle}
 \,d\operatorname{vol}(\phi)
 =(2\pi)^{N/2}\ue^{-\cW(U)}.}
Tonelli's theorem applies to the nonnegative joint density.
Integrating first over #{\phi} proves the normalization and marginal
formulas. Dividing the fixed-#{U} matter density by this Gaussian
integral yields #{K_U}. Multiplying the two displayed densities
recovers the joint density, proving the disintegration identity
##{\mathbb P(dU,d\phi)=\mathbb P_U(dU)K_U(d\phi).}
This explicitly chosen kernel supplies a conditional version at every
connection.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{MeasureTheory.lintegral_prod} states Tonelli's
identity for an almost-everywhere measurable nonnegative extended-real
function, with an s-finite inner measure. The gauge probability and
finite-dimensional Lebesgue measure satisfy these measure conditions;
the density here is measurable. This is partial support for the
product-integration step, not for the Gaussian evaluation or the
marginal and conditional formulas derived above.}

\p{The [selected Gaussian marginal](fdip-005B) and
[conditional propagator](fdip-0060) formulas therefore apply separately
at each fixed #{U}. Their coordinate form is \ref{fdip-00M5}.
These statements keep the connection in the conditioning data.}

\p{After averaging over #{U}, the matter law is generally a mixture
of Gaussian laws, rather than one Gaussian law. Its covariance is
##{\mathbb E_{\mathbb P_U}[Q_U^{-1}],}
by the conditional covariance identity and the zero conditional mean.
Observing retained matter coordinates also reweights the distribution
of #{U} by their conditional likelihood. One cannot obtain the joint
conditional law by keeping the original connection probabilities
fixed and applying a single Gaussian formula.}
